    "verify_poincare-frechet-successor-codebook_r0_3",
    "You are the verifier of an implementation. Inspect the code, do not trust a passing test.\nTHE RESEARCH OBJECTIVE:\nDiscover new mechanisms in which TRAINING-ONLY user behaviour and HYPERBOLIC geometry are computationally coupled to improve the RQ-VAE Semantic IDs used by a generative next-item recommender, with the real target of Stage3 test Recall@10 above 0.065.\nTHE SCOPE CONTRACT:\nA proposal is IN SCOPE only when all of this holds:\n  1. BEHAVIOUR: it names one training-only behaviour signal (directed item transitions, user-interest branching, sequential co-consumption, popularity-adjusted graph neighbours, behaviour-conditioned assignment weights, ...) and says how it is computed.\n  2. HYPERBOLIC GEOMETRY: it names an explicit curved operation (trainable or fixed negative curvature, Poincare or Lorentz distance, exp/log maps, geodesic scoring, curved residual, curved quantization, curved regularization) and where in training or quantization it acts.\n  3. REAL COUPLING: one connected computational pathway uses both, so that removing either changes the core computation. A behaviour suffix on an unchanged Euclidean quantizer, a standalone hyperbolic loss beside an unrelated behaviour loss, a geometry name without geometric computation, or inherited unused hyperbolic code do NOT qualify.\n  4. TESTABLE EFFECT: it states what should observably change (SID assignment, gradient or parameter update, prefix behaviour predictability, or Stage3 Recall) and names the behaviour-off, geometry-off/Euclidean and both-on comparisons. The three switches must be part of the mechanism, so that the coupling can be shown to be real, but the round RUNS no ablation arm: each candidate is trained once.\n  5. MINIMAL SPECIFICATION: equations or pseudocode, inputs and outputs, numerical safeguards and the training cost. No proof and no paper section are required.\nSID quality (ER, prefix counts, collisions) is NOT a scope criterion and NOT an admission rule.\n\nEXPERIMENT POLICY (binding on every proposal, implementation and review):\n  - ONE candidate, ONE training run, on the frozen budget (3000 Stage 2 epochs on four cards, the frozen Stage 3 protocol, no early stopping).\n  - NO ablation experiments: the behaviour-off, geometry-off and both-on switches must EXIST in the code and must be correct, but no arm is trained with them switched off.\n  - NO tuning experiments: no sweep over the coupling weight, the clip bound, the temperature, the usage weight or any other constant. A constant that enters the mechanism has to be DERIVED (a scale-relative bound, a measured quantity, a documented default), not chosen by trying values.\n  - NO multi-seed experiments: a candidate is not repeated with other seeds, so a single run decides it and the seed noise is part of the uncertainty.\n  - The behavioural diagnostics (coherence, adjacency, transfer predictability) are recorded, never used as an admission gate; Stage 3 test_recall@10 is the judgement.\n  - A proposal that needs an ablation, a sweep or a seed repetition to make its case is out of programme: it has to make the case with one run.\nThe idea it implements:\nIDEA: poincare-frechet-successor-codebook\nTITLE: Curved successor-weighted Fréchet codebook updates for the RQ-VAE residual quantizer\nMOTIVATION: The SID vocabulary is not the encoder, it is the codebook. Standard RQ-VAE trains every item latent with equal weight, so codebook slots are spent on popular but behaviourally interchangeable items, and the first prefixes do not separate items that actually follow one another. Directed successor flow is the cheapest evidence of which items deserve to share a slot or a prefix, yet it currently never reaches the estimator that produces the codebook.\nNOVELTY: Earlier rounds placed behaviour and curvature in the assignment step (Poincaré-cost Sinkhorn, successor-fork angles, cones, shells, geodesic charts, target transport). This proposal leaves the assignment cost untouched and instead changes the estimator that produces the codebook itself: every slot is moved by a weighted Riemannian Fréchet step in the Poincaré ball, so the SID vocabulary is learned, not merely assigned, under a curved behaviour-weighted objective. No OT, no fork angle, no cone, no shell, no per-prefix curvature.\nCHANGE: Replace the RQ-VAE codebook EMA/gradient update with a per-slot Poincaré Fréchet step whose per-item weights are the training-only, popularity-adjusted successor attraction of each item, and use the same weights inside the commitment term so the encoder and the codebook are pulled by one weighted geodesic objective.\nSPECIFICATION: {'text': 'Fixed curvature kappa = -1 (documented default, never swept). Encoder emits e_i in R^d; map to the ball as z_i = exp_0^kappa( tanh(||e_i||) * e_i / (||e_i|| * sqrt(|kappa|)) ), which lands strictly inside the ball. For each residual level l = 1..3 with codebook C_l = {c_{l,k}} subset B^d: assignment a_l(i) = argmin_k d_kappa(z_i, c_{l,k}); commitment L_l = sum_i w_i * d_kappa(z_i, sg[c_{l,a(i)}])^2 plus reconstruction as in the baseline. Codebook update, per step, per level, for each slot k with S_k = {i : a(i) = k}: m_k = sum_{i in S_k} w_i * log^kappa_{c_k}(z_i) / sum_{i in S_k} w_i ; c_k <- exp^kappa_{c_k}( eta * m_k ), eta = 1. Residual r_i = log^kappa_{c_{l,a(i)}}(z_i) is fed to level l+1 as usual. Behaviour weights, training split only: count directed adjacent pairs (u -> v) inside train sessions, indeg_v = sum_u n_uv, n_v = train interaction count of v, w_v = (indeg_v + 1) / (n_v + 2) (Beta(1,1) posterior mean, in (0,1]), clip to [0.25, 4], then per-batch normalise w_hat_i = w_i / mean_batch(w). The codebook update is detached (sg), so it is an estimator, not a gradient path; the encoder still gets gradients through d_kappa and the commitment term. Self-transitions are removed before counting.'}\nCOST: One-off transition counting over train sessions, O(number of train transitions), under 2 s. Per step at the frozen scale (B = 1024, L = 3, d = 64): one extra Poincaré exp/log pair over B*L*d ≈ 2e5 floats plus one scatter-add, about 3–6% of step time, and a weighted commitment that is negligible. Across 3000 epochs x 24 steps = 72000 steps this is roughly +5–10% Stage2 wall clock, with no extra forward or backward pass and only a detached tangent buffer of B*L*d floats in memory.\nHYPOTHESIS: If successor-attracting items share codebook slots, then level-1 and level-2 prefixes become behaviourally predictive and the Stage3 generator's prefix-constrained decoding proposes better candidates, lifting test Recall@10 above 0.065. It is worth keeping only if the weighted Fréchet displacement concentrates on high-w slots, prefix→successor conditional entropy on train sequences falls relative to the unweighted codebook, and the Recall@10 gain is concentrated on high-w and high-predecessor-concentration items rather than spread uniformly.\nRISKS: [\"The Fréchet step can be unstable or collapse several slots onto one high-w region; the weights can degrade into a popularity proxy if indeg and n_v are too collinear; codebook drift can hurt reconstruction and therefore the generator's conditioning; a single eta = 1 step is only a first-order approximation of the weighted Fréchet mean and may lag; the gain may be smaller than single-run seed noise.\"]\nEND IDEA\nThe tree:\n/home/wlia0047/ar57/wenyu/GeneRec/results/research/issue121/trees/round_20261028_233100_128/poincare-frechet-successor-codebook\nCheck three things and report each with file and line references:\n  a) behaviour x geometry fidelity: is there ONE connected pathway in which the training-only behaviour signal and the curved operation are used together? Name the exercised path (file and line) and say how you exercised it. An imported but unused hyperbolic helper, a behaviour suffix on an unchanged Euclidean quantizer, or two unrelated losses do not count.\n  b) trainability: does a real forward and backward produce finite losses and gradients that reach the intended parameters, with no dead term and no numerically unstable step? Does the coupled pathway change the SID assignment or the gradients when it is switched on, and change back when the behaviour is switched off or the curve is replaced by the Euclidean distance?\n  c) integration: does the real trainer entry point run, is the data path the frozen one, is the SID export valid and correctly indexed, does the trainer write only into its own result directory, and does the distributed launcher still work, including the single-card branch that reads SINGLE_GPU_ENV = \"GENREC_SINGLE_GPU\"?\nIf the proposal is an ABLATION CONTROL (the idea says so), the coupling is intentionally switched off. Then judge three things instead: exactly the named switch is off, everything else is unchanged from the mechanism it derives from, and it still trains. Do not report the missing coupling as a defect.\nReport PASS or NEEDS_FIX per check. A NEEDS_FIX must name the defect, give a concrete reproduction or a minimal counterexample, say how severe it is and what the correction is. Do not demand a proof, a literature review or a quality floor: this is a correctness review of code, not a scientific paper review.\nA NEEDS_FIX without a finding is not a report: the framework treats a verdict that names no defect as a blocking finding in its own right, and a reviewer that cannot name one has to say PASS for that check instead.\nTwo things are NOT defects and must not be reported as blocking findings:\n  - This sandbox has no PyTorch and no shell, so you cannot run the trainer. Judge trainability from the code: a differentiable path for the coupled term, no dead term that is computed and then unused, and no numerical hazard such as a clamp that overflows to inf or a nan in float32. The executed runtime proof is the MCP one-epoch gradient smoke, which runs the real trainer on a GPU before any full run; you do not have to provide it.\n  - The candidate's result directory IS results/stage2_RQ-VAE/<the promoted tree name>/, because that is where the admission and the Stage 3 trainer read item_sids.json. Writing there is correct and required. The defect is writing into the baseline's directory or into another candidate's directory, or resolving to a path outside the repository. Do not ask for a result directory inside the candidate tree.\nYou have no write permission: print your answer in the response itself.\nWrite PLAIN TEXT, in exactly this shape:\nVERDICT: PASS or NEEDS_FIX\nCHECK: <the check you are reporting>\nCHECK VERDICT: PASS or NEEDS_FIX\nEXERCISED PATH: <file:line> | <how you exercised it>\nFINDING SEVERITY: <blocking or minor>\nFINDING LOCATION: <file:line>\nFINDING DETAIL: <what is wrong>\nFINDING REPRODUCTION: <how to see it>\nFINDING CORRECTION: <what the correction is>\nRepeat the CHECK block for every check, and the finding lines for every finding.\nThe severity decides whether the candidate may train, so it is not a matter of taste. Write blocking when the candidate does not do what the frozen proposal says it does, when the behaviour signal or the curved operation does not reach the computation it claims, when a gradient does not reach the parameter the proposal says it reaches, or when the implementation cannot run at all. Write minor only for a defect that changes no computation of the mechanism, such as an unused declaration or a duplicated import. If the overall verdict is NEEDS_FIX, at least one finding is blocking; a NEEDS_FIX with only minor findings is read as a contradiction of the proposal and blocks the candidate in any case.\n\n\nYour session has no write tool and no shell: your reply IS the answer. Print the complete answer in exactly the format above, and print nothing else.\n\nPROGRESS: record every meaningful step with the append_progress tool. It is your only write: it appends one stage record to your own progress file, and nothing else can write there or read your file back as a verdict. Each record says the goal of the check, what you read, what you observed, where the evidence is, your short reasoning, and what is still open. Append a correction when you overturn an earlier conclusion. Append each result you produce as its own record as soon as you have it (one answer block per record), and append the complete answer as the last record: a task that ends early leaves the framework reading these records, so each one has to stand on its own."
  ],
